{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>It is what HaShem spoke</b> etc. One needs to know the intent of His saying <b>הוּא</b> ('it'). Second, His saying ״לֵאמֹר״ ('saying') implies that HaShem commanded to say it; if so, why did He not say so until now? And this difficulty resembles the difficulty of the warning about Shabbat, which He did not command in its time until afterward He said (Exodus 16:23): ״הוּא אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר ה׳״ — and there we find sufficient reasons for the matter, for ״לֵאמֹר״ was not said there, as He was precise to say ״הוּא אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר ה׳ שַׁבָּתוֹן״ etc. and did not say ״לֵאמֹר״ — unlike what is before us, where He did say ״לֵאמֹר״." "And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, found for themselves that they said (Torat Kohanim, Shemini, chapter 12): Aaron was standing, bewildered, and saying, ״Woe to me — such is the transgression in my hand and in my sons' hand that this has reached me.״ Moses entered to him and was consoling him; he said to him: ״Aaron my brother, from Sinai it was said to me: I am destined to sanctify the House through a great man, and I would have said — either through me or through you the House is sanctified. Now your sons are found to be greater than me and you״ etc. — end quote. Another matter: ״וַיֹּאמֶר מֹשֶׁה״ etc. ״הוּא אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר״ etc. — this utterance was said to Moses at Sinai, and he did not know it until the event came to his hand; and since etc., Moses said to Aaron: ״Your sons did not die except for the sanctification of the Name of the Place,״ as it says (Exodus 28:43): ״וְנוֹעַדְתִּי שָׁמָּה לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וְנִקְדַּשׁ בִּכְבוֹדִי.״ Once Aaron heard that his sons are known to the Place, he was silent — end quote." "<b>Here,</b> according to the first approach, he explains the word <b>הוּא</b> in this manner: that Aaron was grieving over the cause — namely that he was the cause of his sons' death. Therefore Moses told him that הוּא — this — is the reason for their death, for אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר ה׳ etc., and it was not on account of his sin. And the word הוּא according to this refers back to the cause. And according to a second approach, the word <b>הוּא</b> refers back to the sealed utterance that they did not know; and now he knew that הוּא — this — is the intent of the utterance said to him earlier: ״וְנִקְדַּשׁ בִּכְבוֹדִי״; also, nothing was said there, not a thing and not לֵאמֹר — examine closely and you will find — and the matters remained sealed in Moses' heart until the time of the event, and he understood them and said ״הוּא אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר ה׳ אֵלַי,״ and what did He speak? ״וְנִקְדַּשׁ בִּכְבוֹדִי.״ And His saying <b>לֵאמֹר</b> etc. — meaning: the intent of HaShem's words in saying ״וְנִקְדַּשׁ בִּכְבוֹדִי״ is to say the following: in saying ״וְנִקְדַּשׁ״ His intention is ״בִּקְרֹבַי אֶקָּדֵשׁ,״ and His saying ״בִּכְבוֹדִי״ is ״וְעַל פְּנֵי כָל הָעָם אֶכָּבֵד.״ And according to this approach all the linguistic difficulties are resolved; but according to the first approach the difficulty we raised still remains. And to grasp the depth of his words, one must note his saying ״either through me or through you,״ for he should have said only ״through me or through you,״ or one may say ״through you or through me,״ for it is not proper to say ״or״ at the beginning." "<b>And to this</b> it appears proper to resolve, that since there are in this matter two aspects: the first is the aspect of spiritual rank, and the second is the aspect of what occurs, namely death. And when he came to say what he had understood — namely who is the close one through whom sanctification would come — if he had begun to say ״through me״ or ״through you,״ it would have been heard in his words as though he is the one closest before HaShem in the highest rank and honor. Therefore he mentioned himself with uncertainty and mentioned Aaron with doubt, as ״or.״ For this reason he said ״or through me,״ placing even himself in doubt. And had he said ״through you or through me,״ the suspicion would arise that he wanted to clothe the evil event upon Aaron — that he would die from the indwelling of the Shekhinah — while making himself only a doubt. Therefore Moses acted wisely and said ״or through me,״ placing himself first regarding the aspect of receiving punishment, though not definitively, so as not to enter the suspicion that he esteems himself at a greater level than Aaron; and he said ״or״ by way of doubt, in the same manner of doubt that he expressed regarding Aaron in saying ״or through you.״" "Yet it is still difficult: since from HaShem only one of them was said, and the doubt entered only regarding some one of them, if so why did two die? And further: from where does he derive to say that they are greater than Moses and Aaron, since HaShem said only one of them, and both of them died in place of one of them — so he can only say that the two are better than one of them (Ecclesiastes 4:9)." "However, the intention is that when HaShem said to him ״וְנִקְדַּשׁ בִּכְבוֹדִי״ (Exodus 29:43), Moses knew that there would be a death among the righteous, only he reasoned according to the measure of the implication of the words in His saying ״וְנִקְדַּשׁ בִּכְבוֹדִי״ — that the House would be sanctified through that one who is my glory, my honored one, who is the righteous man. According to this there is only one righteous man here, for so does ״כְּבוֹדִי״ imply — singular language — and the doubt fell to him as to who this singular one is: whether it is Moses or whether it is Aaron, and that is his saying ״either through me or through you,״ as we have explained. And now, when he saw that two had died, he returned to understand the intention of His saying ״וְנִקְדַּשׁ בִּכְבוֹדִי״ — that the intention is not that it shall be sanctified, and through what — בִּכְבוֹדִי, which is singular language; rather the intention is in this manner: ״בִּקְרֹבַי אֶקָּדֵשׁ״ — and that is His saying ״וְנִקְדַּשׁ״; and His saying ״וְעַל פְּנֵי כָל הָעָם אֶכָּבֵד״ is the intention of His saying ״בִּכְבוֹדִי״ — meaning, for My glory. According to this explanation, HaShem did not speak in singular language regarding the righteous, for the righteous were alluded to in His saying ״וְנִקְדַּשׁ,״ and His saying ״בִּכְבוֹדִי״ means for My glory. And the proof that this is indeed the intention of His blessed saying is that behold, two died, and His saying ״הוּא אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר״ is an answer to Aaron, that he should not be distressed, etc. And the explanation of the words of HaShem, which are ״לֵאמֹר,״ means: the intention of His saying is in this manner — ״בִּקְרֹבַי״ etc. and ״וְעַל פְּנֵי״ etc. — to preclude another interpretation that one might be forced to derive from it, namely that not more than one would die." "<b>Or</b> it is possible that Moses also understood this path — that which He said here, ״בִּקְרֹבַי״ etc. ״וְעַל פְּנֵי״ etc. — and this is what he alluded to in his words ״either through me״ etc., for his saying ״אוֹ״ (either) indicates that another division preceded, namely both of them together, and this was the path that ultimately proved correct — either through me alone, or through you alone — and after the fact the balanced doubt was resolved, that the intention was ״בִּקְרֹבַי״ etc. ״וְעַל פְּנֵי״ etc., and therefore two died." "With this the fine points we examined in the verse are settled. And there remains to resolve, from every perspective, who told Moses to decide thus — perhaps the verse is always to be taken at its plain sense, and the House was sanctified through one righteous man alluded to in the word ״כְּבוֹדִי,״ and the reason two died is that the merit of both of them together equals the measure of one of them — Moses or Aaron? Behold, by understanding the reason for the matter we will know that this is no difficulty, for the reason for the matter is on account of HaShem's concern that when Israel saw their Elohim, whose name dwells among them, [they would see that] His greatness and His awe are not recognized in the orderly fashion in which it is recognized among the supernal beings, such that His awe would not be upon them as needed and they would not conduct themselves according to the measure of honor required of them. And there is yet greater reason to be concerned about the great ones of Israel, that they might think in their minds that they are close to HaShem and wish to approach and draw near. Therefore He executed judgment upon the greatest among them all, than whom there is no boundary higher to be concerned about — to say that relative to his station, the drawing near to HaShem would not be withheld from him — and all the rest of the people remained guarding the honor of the Blessed One, as His saying ״וְעַל פְּנֵי כָל הָעָם״ etc. And from now on, when we say that Moses in and of himself is greater than Nadab or Abihu, [and] Aaron in and of himself is also greater than each one of them, if so, HaShem left a level above which there is no [such] awe. And on the side of their joining together, surely the merit and attainment of one does not avail for the other to prepare him for felicity to draw near to the Shekhinah, and the limitation of honor to HaShem is only according to the boundary of each one of them, and not according to a boundary greater than any one of them. And when we negate this distinction and wish to say that the merit of two combines, if so there yet lacks the magnification of His blessed honor relative to Moses and Aaron together, or three as in Nadab and Abihu [plus another], or five, if their combined merit would exceed the merit of Nadab and Abihu — but surely the combining does not apply here. And further you will find that the merit of a single individual is not comparable to the merit of one who is supported in the manner of precious stones and gems — two small ones, even though they may be larger in their combination together than one large one, the large one does not enter with them into an equal valuation, and the matter is straightforward."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The numeral ב׳ ('second') introduces the second difficulty he raises.", 1 "Resolved the rabbinic abbreviation רז״ל as 'our Rabbis, of blessed memory' per standard convention. The citation תורת כהנים הכא normalized; the 'שם' reference for Exodus 28:43 is understood as pointing back to that earlier-cited verse.", 2 "No translator's note needed.", 4 "The citation from Ecclesiastes 4:9 (״טוֹבִים הַשַּׁנַיִם מִן הָאֶחָד״) is applied here as a logical inference rather than a direct quotation of the verse's original context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:55:34.543165177Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}